<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
         https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>com.xkondix</groupId>
        <artifactId>ai-agents-JAVA-SPRING</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>multimodal-lab</artifactId>
    <name>Multimodal Lab - one router, four models, routed by tool choice</name>
    <description>
        The only module in this project that is NOT implemented twice.

        A cheap router agent that never sees a pixel or a byte: every
        modality is a @Tool, and each tool talks to its own model. "Here is a
        photo of the 2007 squad — draw a modern kit for them and read the
        result out loud" becomes analyze_image, then generate_image, then
        speak_text, in an order nobody wrote down.

        The multi-step part is free: in Spring AI 2.0 tool execution lives in
        ToolCallingAdvisor, which keeps calling the model until it stops
        asking for tools. There is no hand-written loop here — unlike
        patterns-spring-ai, where the loop IS the point.

        SPRING AI ONLY, ON PURPOSE. Everywhere else the same thing is built
        twice to compare the frameworks. Here that comparison would be
        dishonest: Spring AI ships ImageModel, TextToSpeechModel and
        TranscriptionModel as first-class abstractions and LangChain4j has no
        equivalent audio story. This is where the two stop being
        interchangeable — a more useful slide than a sixth side-by-side.

        Port: 8089
    </description>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- One starter covers all four modalities. Verified in Spring AI
             2.0.0: OpenAiImageModel, OpenAiAudioSpeechModel (implements
             TextToSpeechModel) and OpenAiAudioTranscriptionModel (implements
             TranscriptionModel) all live in spring-ai-openai and all build
             their client through OpenAiSetup, which honours base-url — so
             they route to OpenRouter exactly like chat does. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.ai</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-ai-starter-model-openai</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Chat memory in Redis rather than in a field.
             Conversations here carry references to files on disk, so losing
             them on restart is worse than in the other modules: the artifacts
             survive and the thread that produced them does not. The starter
             pulls spring-boot-starter-data-redis; the container is already in
             docker-compose on 6379. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.ai</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-ai-starter-model-chat-memory-repository-redis</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Actuator: endpoints AND the condition that enables OTLP log export
             (ConditionalOnEnabledLoggingExport). Do not remove. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
            <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.xkondix</groupId>
            <artifactId>common</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
